<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domoni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domoni is a town on the east coast of Anjouan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>. It was the early capital of the Anjouan sultanate founded by Shirazi-descended rulers around 1500, and it keeps old stone houses, mosques and tombs, though the ruling house later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsamudu">Mutsamudu</a>. It is the home town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-abdallah">Ahmed Abdallah</a>, first president of the Comoros.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Domoni lies on the east coast of Anjouan, also called Nzwani, and is the island's second largest town, with a population of 15,351. It is one of the island's three major towns, with Bambao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsamudu">Mutsamudu</a>, and is linked to Mutsamudu by a road of about 16 kilometres. The old town is divided into three neighbourhoods: Hari ya muji, Maweni and Momoni. The middle section, Hari ya Muzhi, has been the centre of activity since the 16th century and holds the main plaza, the Friday mosque and many mansions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to archaeological finds the town was established in the 12th century. By the 15th century it was a flourishing trading centre, with links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Arab lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and artefacts show contact with places as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>The navigator Ibn Majid, who had guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, confirmed the port as a major trading centre. In the 16th century many chiefs, known as Feni, controlled different parts of the island. Domoni was the capital of the Anjouan sultanate until the end of the 18th century, when Mwenye Fani, who took the name Abdallah I on becoming sultan, moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsamudu">Mutsamudu</a>. It has a significant Shirazi population, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> aristocratic immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> who arrived between the 14th and 18th centuries.</p><p>Infighting among the chiefs of Anjouan led them to ask Europeans to broker peace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> became a French protectorate in 1886, and in 1909 the French made them part of their own country. In 1975 the three islands formed the Republic of the Comoro Islands. Its first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-abdallah">Ahmed Abdallah</a>, came from Domoni; he was assassinated in 1989 by a presidential guard, and his white mausoleum, with four tall minarets, stands in the town. Tadjidine ben Saïd Massonde was also born there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The first stone building in Domoni dates from the mid-13th century, and the old houses built by the Shirazi migrants can still be seen. The mosques, built of stone masonry on raised foundations, date from the eleventh and twelfth centuries, were rebuilt in the thirteenth and enlarged in the fourteenth and fifteenth. The Shirazi Mosque has two mihrabs where most have one, and the Friday mosque has the tallest minaret on the island. The town is known for mansions of the 16th and 18th centuries with richly carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> doors, and for its wood carving and embroidery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domoni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shirazi-descended families established a sultanate on Anjouan (Ndzuwani) around 1500, with an early capital at <b>Domoni</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-anjouan">Sultanate of Anjouan</a></li></ul></section>
